DAY 2: SSH Key-Based Authentication & Disabling Passwords

Goal: Ditch plain-text password authentication for SSH, set up asymmetric cryptographic keys, and lock down the daemon so passwords can't even be attempted.
What I Did

    Generated an Ed25519 Key Pair (Host):
    Ran ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "flow-abdcomputar-key" on my host machine.

        Chosen Ed25519 over RSA because it’s faster, modern, and uses shorter, super-secure elliptic curve keys.

        Added a strong passphrase to encrypt the private key file (~/.ssh/id_ed25519) on disk.

    Deployed Public Key to VM:
    Used ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub abdullahthexpert@192.168.1.5 to append my public key straight into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on abdcomputar.

        Lesson learned on syntax: Caught myself trying ~./ssh and ./ssh when checking the files. Path syntax matters: ~/.ssh is the hidden directory inside my home folder.

        Confirmed permissions auto-set to 0600 (-rw-------), meaning only my user can read/write to authorized_keys.

    Hardened SSH Configuration (/etc/ssh/sshd_config):
    Uncommented and edited key security directives in the SSH daemon config using nano:

        PasswordAuthentication no — stops the server from accepting password-based logins.

        PubkeyAuthentication yes — enforces key-based logins.

        KbdInteractiveAuthentication no — blocks PAM challenge-response password fallbacks.

    Tested & Verified (The Zero-Lockout Habit):

        Ran sudo sshd -t to test syntax before restarting — returned zero errors (silence = success).

        Applied changes with sudo systemctl restart ssh.

        Audited active runtime settings using sudo sshd -T | grep -E -i "passwordauthentication|kbdinteractiveauthentication" to confirm both flags were explicitly set to no.

        The Final Proof Test: Forced a password-only attempt with no identity keys attached:
        ssh -i /dev/null -o PubkeyAuthentication=no abdullahthexpert@192.168.1.5
        Result: Permission denied (publickey). Total lockdown achieved.

Security Takeaways

    Asymmetric Cryptography: The private key (id_ed25519) never leaves my host computer. The server only holds the public key (authorized_keys). During login, the server sends a challenge encrypted with the public key, which my host proves it can solve with the matching private key. Passwords never cross the wire.

    Key Passphrases: Encrypting the private key with a passphrase adds a 2nd factor: even if someone steals my private key file, it's useless without my passphrase.

    Preventing Overrides: Always check runtime configuration with sshd -T to ensure drop-in configs in /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/ aren't secretly overriding main security rules.
